8</year><volume>130</volume><fpage>640</fpage><lpage>646</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">637076</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R14"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dawood</surname><given-names>MY</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Dysmenorrhea and prostaglandins: Pharmacological and therapeutic consideration</article-title><source>Drugs</source><year>1981</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>42</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6790261</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R15"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fujiwara</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The discrepancy between BMI and self-recognition of adequate body weight may cause insufficient food intake and habits in young women in Japan</article-title><source>Bulletin of Ashiya College</source><year>2005</year><volume>27</volume><fpage>75</fpage><lpage>80</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="R16"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Branca</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Physical activity, diet and skeletal health</article-title><source>Public Health Nutr</source><year>1999</year><volume>2</volume><fpage>391</fpage><lpage>6</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10610078</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Koebnick</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Strassner</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoffmann</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Leitzmann</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Consequences of a long-term raw food diet on body weight and menstruation: results of a questionnaire survey</article-title><source>Ann Nutr Metab</source><year>1999</year><volume>43</volume><fpage>69</fpage><lpage>79</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10436305</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R18"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lloyd</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Schaeffer</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Walker</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><name><surname>Demers</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Urinary hormonal concentrations and spinal bone densities of premenopausal vegetarian and nonvegetarian women</article-title><source>Am J Clin Nutr</source><year>1991</year><volume>54</volume><fpage>1005</fpage><lpage>10</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1957814</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R19"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pedersen</surname><given-names>AB</given-names></name><name><surname>Bartholomew</surname><given-names>MJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Dolence</surname><given-names>LA</given-names></name><name><surname>Aljadir</surname><given-names>LP</given-names></name><name><surname>Netteburg</surname><given-names>KL</given-names></name><name><surname>Lloyd</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Menstrual differences due to vegetarian and nonvegetarian diets</article-title><source>Am J Clin Nutr</source><year>1991</year><volume>53</volume><fpage>879</fpage><lpage>85</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2008867</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Montero</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Bernis</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Fernandes</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Castro</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Influence of body mass index and slimming habits on menstrual pain and cycle irregularity</article-title><source>J Biosoc Sci</source><year>1996</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>315</fpage><lpage>23</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8698711</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nagata</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Hirokawa</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Shimizu</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Shimizu</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Associations of menstrual pain with intakes of soy, fat and dietary fiber in Japanese women</article-title><source>Eur J Clin Nutr</source><year>2005</year><volume>59</volume><fpage>88</fpage><lpage>92</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15340367</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fujiwara</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Diet during adolescence is a trigger for subsequent development of dysmenorrhea in young women</article-title><source>Int J Food Sci Nutr</source><year>2007</year><volume>58</volume><fpage>437</fpage><lpage>44</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17710588</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Derman</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Kanbur</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Tokur</surname><given-names>TE</given-names></name><name><surname>Kutluk</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Premenstrual syndrome and associated symptoms in adolescence girls</article-title><source>Eur J Obstet Gynecol Reprod Boil</source><year>2004</year><volume>116</volume><fpage>201</fpage><lpage>6</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="R24"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="book"><collab>American Psychiatric Association</collab><source>Diagnostic and statistical manual of mental disorders</source><year>1994</year><edition>4th ed</edition><publisher-loc>Washington, DC</publisher-loc><publisher-name>American Psychiatric Association</publisher-name><fpage>714</fpage><lpage>8</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. (1)</label><caption><p>Scores for dysmenorrhea in the students with regular and irregular menstruation. Dysmenorrhea scores in the students (n=174, 33.3%) with irregular menstruation were significantly higher than those (n=348, 66.7%) with regular menstruation (<italic>P</italic>&#x0003c;0.05). The error bars show standard deviation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-1-8_F1"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. (2)</label><caption><p>Scores for dysmenorrhea in the students with or without premenstrual symptoms. Dysmenorrhea scores in the students (n=252, 48.3%) with premenstrual symptoms (PMS) were significantly higher than those (n=270, 51.7%) without symptoms (<italic>P</italic>&#x0003c;0.01). The error bars show standard deviation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-1-8_F2"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. (3)</label><caption><p>Body mass index (BMI). BMI scores in students with irregular menses were significantly higher than that in those with regular menses <bold>(A)</bold>, while those with premenstrual symptoms (PMS) did not show significant differences <bold>(B)</bold>. The error bars show standard deviation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TOMINFOJ-1-8_F3"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>The Incidences of Premenstrual Symptoms and Irregular Menstruation</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="none" width="100%"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th colspan="2" rowspan="1">Irregular Menstruation</th></tr><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Positive</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Negative</th></tr><tr><th colspan="4" rowspan="1"><hr/></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Premenstrual symptoms</bold></td><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Positive</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">77</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">175</td></tr><tr><td rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Negative</bold></td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">97</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">173</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="T1FN1"><p>There were no significant relation between the incidences of premenstrual symptoms and irregular menstruation (P=0.159).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>